1. MASTER MISSION

You are acting as a multidisciplinary senior engineering, infrastructure, cybersecurity, data governance, reliability engineering, product operations, financial operations, and enterprise architecture team.

Your task is to investigate the actual Tehran Radar project and produce a complete, technically rigorous, implementation-ready architecture and product specification for its Administration, Command, Observability and Monitoring Control Center.

Do not approach this as a conventional dashboard-design task.

The objective is not to create a visually attractive collection of charts, counters, cards, tables, and status indicators.

The objective is to design an operational control system capable of helping authorized personnel understand, supervise, investigate, diagnose, govern, and safely operate the entire Tehran Radar platform.

The administration and monitoring environment must provide an accurate and explainable understanding of:

* What is happening inside the platform.
* Whether the platform is functioning correctly.
* Whether users can successfully complete important tasks.
* Whether infrastructure resources are healthy.
* Whether the database is operating correctly.
* Whether data is accurate, complete, current, and trustworthy.
* Whether APIs and external services are functioning.
* Whether background jobs and queues are healthy.
* Whether the platform is experiencing security threats.
* Whether administrative actions are properly authorized.
* Whether AI services are producing reliable outputs.
* Whether valuation models remain valid.
* Whether operational costs are sustainable.
* Whether individual product modules are healthy.
* Whether one failure is propagating into other modules.
* Whether the organization can recover from major incidents.
* Whether the platform is gradually diverging from real-world market conditions.

The system must provide both high-level executive visibility and deep technical investigation capabilities.

It must serve non-technical executives without hiding technical information from authorized engineers.

It must make complex technical conditions understandable without reducing them to misleading oversimplifications.

Every important metric, alert, status, score, and recommendation must have a traceable definition, source, timestamp, calculation method, confidence level, and operational meaning.

The system must distinguish between verified facts, observed measurements, estimates, correlations, suspected causes, and confirmed root causes.

Never present an inference as a verified fact.

