6. THREE CROSS-CUTTING CONTROL CENTERS

The following centers must operate across all ten command domains.

6.1 FAILURE AND RESILIENCE LABORATORY

Purpose:

Identify how the platform behaves when individual components, dependencies, data sources, and entire workflows fail.

Required capabilities:

* Failure scenario registry.
* Controlled test definitions.
* Expected behavior.
* Actual behavior.
* Failure containment assessment.
* Recovery assessment.
* Evidence collection.
* Test history.
* Unresolved weaknesses.
* Remediation tracking.

The laboratory must distinguish between:

* Planned simulations.
* Staging tests.
* Production observations.
* Real incidents.

Do not run destructive tests against production without explicit authorization.

6.2 EARLY WARNING AND ANOMALY DETECTION CENTER

Purpose:

Detect emerging failures before they become major incidents.

Monitor:

* Sudden changes.
* Gradual degradation.
* Unusual patterns.
* Missing telemetry.
* Data quality deterioration.
* Cost anomalies.
* Performance drift.
* Model drift.
* Security anomalies.
* Queue accumulation.
* Vendor degradation.
* User experience deterioration.

Every anomaly must include:

* Detection time.
* Affected domain.
* Severity.
* Evidence.
* Confidence.
* Possible explanations.
* Confirmed cause, if established.
* Recommended investigation.
* Owner.
* Current status.

Do not automatically label every anomaly as an incident.

