7. ADMINISTRATION CONTROL PRINCIPLES

7.1 Least privilege

Every administrative function must be protected by explicit permissions.

Do not use a single unrestricted administrator role for all functions.

Permissions must distinguish:

* View.
* Investigate.
* Export.
* Propose.
* Approve.
* Execute.
* Configure.
* Disable.
* Delete.
* Recover.

High-risk actions must require additional controls.

7.2 Separation of duties

For sensitive operations, separate:

* Requester.
* Reviewer.
* Approver.
* Executor.
* Auditor.

A single person should not be able to silently request, approve, execute, and erase evidence of a high-risk operation.

7.3 Time-limited privileged access

Privileged access should be granted for a defined purpose and duration where technically feasible.

The system must record:

* Who received access.
* Why access was granted.
* Who approved it.
* Which permissions were granted.
* When access started.
* When access expired.
* Whether access was revoked.
* Which actions occurred during the session.

7.4 Immutable or independently protected audit evidence

Administrative audit records must be protected from ordinary administrative modification or deletion.

The control center must identify whether audit integrity is independently protected or merely stored alongside the application data.

Do not claim audit immutability unless the actual storage and access architecture supports that claim.

7.5 No hidden administrative backdoors

Do not design undocumented administrative accounts, bypass endpoints, secret master passwords, or unlogged emergency controls.

Emergency access must be explicit, restricted, time-limited, logged, and independently reviewable.

